<!--
  SwathKeeper — Fast DDS transport profile (lever L2, round 3, 2026-08-22).

  WHAT THIS FIXES, and why it is not a QoS change.
  Every large sample on this stack fragments at 65,384 B *even over shared memory*: the SHM
  transport's own max_message_size defaults to 65500, UDPv4 is hard-capped at 65500, and a
  participant takes the MINIMUM max_message_size across its registered transports. Fast DDS 2.6.11's
  default SHM segment is 512 KiB (patched in at SharedMemTransport::init, the descriptor default is
  literally 0), which holds exactly EIGHT of those fragments — 524288 / 65384 = 8.02. Anything
  needing more overflows, and on overflow Fast DDS DISCARDS the fragment and reports success:
  alloc_buffer ignores the blocking deadline, throws, and the transport catch logs at logInfo, which
  is compiled out of release builds. Silent loss, no counter, no warning.

  That model predicts every number this project has measured, monotonically in fragment count:
    camera_info  ~500 B      =  1 fragment  (fits)  -> 100 %  delivered   (the control)
    nir mono16    614,400 B  = 10 fragments > 8     ->  46.2 % delivered
    rgb8          921,600 B  = 15 fragments > 8     ->  20.5 % delivered
    ndvi 32FC1  1,228,800 B  = 19 fragments > 8     ->  37.5 % delivered pre-RELIABLE, 100 % after
  Implied per-fragment loss is a consistent 5-10 % across all three bands. The /fg/ndvi/image result
  is the control experiment: RELIABLE (lever L1) did not make the transport faster, it added
  NACK_FRAG retransmission of the fragments the segment was dropping.

  THE CHANGE: segment_size 512 KiB -> 8 MiB = 128 fragment slots (16x), ~5 sensor ticks of buffering
  against the bridge's ~25 fragments/tick. maxMessageSize stays at 65500 ON PURPOSE — raising it
  does nothing while UDPv4 is registered (the min-across-transports rule), and segment_size must
  stay ABOVE max_message_size or SharedMemTransport::init logs an error and rejects the descriptor.
  Zero-fragmentation requires dropping UDPv4 entirely; that is a separate, higher-risk lever (L4)
  and is deliberately NOT in this file.

  port_queue_capacity is deliberately left at its 512 default: the 8-slot segment is the binding
  constraint, and bundling a second SHM knob would make a landed win unattributable.

  HOW IT IS LOADED: FASTRTPS_DEFAULT_PROFILES_FILE=/workspace/fieldguard/config/dds/fg_fastdds.xml
  (the repo is bind-mounted read/write, same precedent as config/sitl_params/dds_udp.parm). The env
  var must reach EVERY process that creates a Fast DDS participant — `bash -c` is non-interactive and
  never sources .bashrc, so an rc-file export reaches nothing.

  VERIFY IT ACTUALLY LOADED — malformed XML falls back to defaults with only a log line:
    ls -l /dev/shm/fastrtps_* | grep -v port
  Default segment files measure 549,408 B (512 KiB + ~25 KB header). With this profile they must
  measure ~8.4 MB, and the MINIMUM must be raised too — a 549,408 B segment still in the set means
  some participant missed the env var. `dds_env_snapshot()` reports exactly those two numbers into
  every clip's meta["dds"] (schema 1.4).

  SIZING: Fast DDS memsets the whole segment at init, so each participant costs its full
  segment_size in /dev/shm. 6 participants x 8 MiB = 48 MB, which does NOT fit the stock 64 MB
  /dev/shm alongside everything else — scripts/sim_docker_run.sh raises the container's shm-size to
  1g for this reason. The failure mode if it does not fit is participant-creation failure at
  bringup: loud and safe, not slow. (No double hyphen anywhere in this comment on purpose: it is
  illegal inside an XML comment and would make the whole profile unparseable, which Fast DDS
  reports by falling back to defaults with only a log line.)
-->
<dds xmlns="http://www.eprosima.com">
  <profiles>
    <transport_descriptors>

      <transport_descriptor>
        <transport_id>fg_shm</transport_id>
        <type>SHM</type>
        <!-- 8 MiB = 128 fragment slots at 65,384 B, against 8 in the 512 KiB default. -->
        <segment_size>8388608</segment_size>
        <!-- Left at the default. Raising it is inert while UDPv4 is registered (min across
             transports) and would break the segment_size > max_message_size invariant if raised
             past 8 MiB. -->
        <maxMessageSize>65500</maxMessageSize>
      </transport_descriptor>

      <!-- UDPv4 is RETAINED. useBuiltinTransports=false replaces the whole default transport set,
           so without this descriptor the participant would become SHM-only — which is lever L4, a
           different experiment with a different risk profile (no UDP locator means any process that
           misses the env var cannot reach these participants AT ALL, and the container's DDS graph
           stops being reachable from outside). Keeping UDPv4 here means a process that misses the
           profile still communicates, just over UDP: this lever DEGRADES, it does not black out. -->
      <transport_descriptor>
        <transport_id>fg_udp</transport_id>
        <type>UDPv4</type>
      </transport_descriptor>

    </transport_descriptors>

    <participant profile_name="fg_default" is_default_profile="true">
      <rtps>
        <userTransports>
          <transport_id>fg_shm</transport_id>
          <transport_id>fg_udp</transport_id>
        </userTransports>
        <!-- Required: otherwise the builtin UDPv4/SHM pair is registered IN ADDITION to the
             descriptors above, and the default-sized builtin SHM transport would be the one that
             carries the data. -->
        <useBuiltinTransports>false</useBuiltinTransports>
      </rtps>
    </participant>

  </profiles>
</dds>
